@title What Eighteen Months of Four-Day Weeks Did to Our Support Team
@author Priscilla Adeyemi-Koh, Head of Customer Support
@subtitle Halden Cartography blog

For eighteen months, the support team at Halden has worked four days a week. We are now making it permanent, and I want to write down what that actually looked like, including the parts that went badly, because the glowing version is not very useful to anyone trying to decide whether to do this themselves. Some of what follows will sound like a success story, and parts of it are. Other parts are not.

# Why we tried it
Our support team has 23 people. In 2024, we lost 21 percent of them over the year, and when we asked why people were leaving, burnout was the reason we heard most. That number bothered me more than any other in our reports. Every departure meant weeks of training for someone new and a little more pressure on the people who stayed, and we were stuck in a loop of hiring to keep up.

A four-day week was not a clever idea we went looking for. It was the most direct answer we could think of to the thing people were telling us. We did not know whether it would work, so we treated it as a pilot, and we started on 2025-04-07.

I should admit what we did not expect. We thought the hard part would be the schedule itself. It turned out the harder part was the commitments around it, with customers and other teams who were used to five days of coverage. Looking back, the decision felt less like a bold experiment and more like a fix to a problem we had been living with for years.

# How the schedule works
Everyone works Monday to Thursday, nine hours a day. That is 36 hours a week, at full pay. The queue still needed to be covered on Fridays, so what changed was the shape of the week, not the amount of coverage.

Friday is the hard part, so we handled it separately. Coverage on Fridays comes from a rotating on-call group of four people. Each person in that group takes a Monday off instead. We kept that group small on purpose, because a rotation that is too large makes it hard for anyone to feel responsible for a Friday. We wrote the coverage rules down before the first week started, including who covers Friday and how handoffs work, because deciding those things on the fly in a busy week is how a pilot falls apart.

# What happened to the numbers
Some numbers moved the way we had hoped. Median first response time fell from 3.4 hours to 2.9 hours. Customer satisfaction rose from 4.3 to 4.5 out of 5. Annual attrition dropped from 21 percent to 9 percent, and sick days fell by 31 percent.

I want to be careful with those. A team of 23 can swing a lot on a few departures, and I would not want anyone to read our attrition figure as a law of nature. What convinced me was seeing the customer numbers and the team numbers move in the same direction at once. Customers got slightly faster and happier answers, while the people giving those answers were less likely to leave and took fewer sick days.

Numbers like these are easy to over-read, so I tried to hold them loosely. A better week for a support team is one where people go home on time and customers still get answers. We were glad to see both happen, and we were careful not to claim more than that.

# What got harder
It was not all smooth. Thursday evenings became the hardest part of the week. Tickets in progress had to be handed to the Friday on-call group, and handoffs are where things get dropped. We improved our handoff notes, but I would not say we solved it.

Two enterprise customers asked for guaranteed phone support on Fridays. We could have said no. Instead we hired two more people to cover it, which was a real cost and a decision I still think about. And a few people told us that nine-hour days were tiring in winter, when the days are short and dark. We took that seriously and we are still talking about it. The winter feedback was the one I liked least to hear, because it was the one problem a schedule cannot fix with a rule. A nine-hour day is still a nine-hour day in January.

# If you try it
Three things I would tell anyone considering this.

1. Pilot for at least six months. Two months is long enough to feel excited and not long enough to see a winter.
2. Write the coverage rules before the first week, not during it.
3. Measure customer metrics alongside team morale. If you only watch how people feel, you can miss a service that is quietly getting worse. If you only watch the service, you can miss people burning out.

None of this is a magic answer. A four-day week fixed some of our problems and created others, and the honest version of this story names both. We made the decision to keep it on 2026-10-02, after eighteen months of data. I am glad we tested it before committing, and I am glad we measured both sides.
